@page "/accessibility"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]
@using ArchitectCoach.Web.Services.Privacy
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Options
@inject IOptions<SiteOptions> SiteOptionsAccessor

<PageTitle>Accessibility · ArchitectCoach</PageTitle>

<article class="col-lg-9">
    <h1>Accessibility statement</h1>

    <p>
        We want ArchitectCoach to work for everyone. Our target is
        <strong>WCAG 2.2 level AA</strong>, the standard used for ADA and European Accessibility Act compliance.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h4">What we do</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Every page works with a keyboard alone, with a visible focus indicator and a "skip to main content" link.</li>
        <li>Pages use real headings, landmarks, labeled form fields, and table headers, so screen readers can navigate them.</li>
        <li>Text and controls meet AA contrast ratios, and meaning is never shown by color alone (for example, every badge says "Low complexity" in words).</li>
        <li>Pages reflow to a phone-width screen and 200% zoom without sideways scrolling, and respect your "reduce motion" setting.</li>
        <li>Sign-in allows pasting and password managers, with no puzzles or CAPTCHAs.</li>
        <li>Every page is checked automatically on each change: axe-core WCAG 2.2 AA rules, keyboard reachability with visible focus, and reflow at phone width. Automated checks can't catch everything, so we also test by hand with a screen reader.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="h4">Known issues</h2>
    <p>None known right now. If you run into a barrier, we want to hear about it.</p>

    <h2 class="h4">Report a problem</h2>
    <p>
        Email <strong>@(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(SiteOptionsAccessor.Value.PrivacyContactEmail) ? "[not yet configured]" : SiteOptionsAccessor.Value.PrivacyContactEmail)</strong>
        with the page and what went wrong. We aim to reply within 5 business days.
    </p>
</article>
